CMP_Circulars_29102025_638973560481999250_Rabi Minikit 2025-26.pdf_002,other_docs,CMP_Circulars_29102025_638973560481999250_Rabi Minikit 2025-26.pdf,2,"Gram ( 2020 6000 6000 RVG-204) Phule Vikram 2020 2000 2000 BG 3062 2020 9000 9000 JG 24 2020 8000 8000 State Total 25000 Grand Total 172700 6300 21000 200000 -6 Annexure lll State/Crop/Variety/Agency.wise allocation for URAD d urin g Rabi 2025-26 Supply through National Seed Total S.No State Variety (Urad) Year of release Agency Quantity NSC HIL 1 Andhra Pradesh VBN-8 201 8 45000 45000 VBN-,IO 2020 32500 32500 LBG-884 2023 1000 1000 Kota Urd-5 2021 2200 2200 VBN.9 2020 12000 12000 State Total 92700 2 Tamil Nadu VBN-8 2018 39000 39000 VBN.1O 2020 30000 30000 Kota Urd-s 2021 2000 2000 VBN-9 2020 1 1500 11500 State Total 82500 3 Assam Kota Urd4 2020 20000 20000 4 Telanqana Kota Urd-s 2021 4000 4000 5 West Bengal Kota Urd4 2020 7000 7000 6 Karnataka VBN-8 20'18 3000 3000 VBN-10 2020 2000 2000 Kota Urd-s 2021 1000 1000 VBN.9 2020 1000 1000 t-------r------ State Total 7000 7 Odisha VBN.8 2018 500 4000 4500 VBN-10 2020 1000 1000 2000 Kota Urd-s 2021 1000 1000 VBN-9 2020 1000 '1000 State Total 8500 8 Tripu ra PU-10-23 20'19 2000 2000 o Uttar Pradesh Kota Urd4 2020 1 1000 11000 tPU-11-2 2019 '! 5300 15300 State Total 26300 Grand Total 47500 202500 250000 = Annexure-lV SIZE & PRICE OF SEED MINIKITS Minikit Size Rate Crop Rate/Kit (in Kg) (Rs./Kq) Gram 16 108 1728 Urad 4 't27 508 I Lentil 108 864 L> '** I (Dr. Dilip Kr. Srivastava) Dy. Commissioner (Pulses Mission) a) 6 Annerure-V Modalities to be adopted by states and the supplying agencies for minikits distribution as per new guidelines of NFNSM for FY 2025-26: The Terms and Conditions for Minikits distribution is applicable as per the new operational guidelines of National Food Security & Nutrition Mission (NFSNM) 2025-26 given at point No. 2 (C) (i) to 2 (C) (xvi) in page No, 26 lo 29 of new guidelines. ln addition, the competent authority has approved following conditions: 1) The Seed lnventory Module developed by Seeds Division of DA&FW is to be used for recording and supply of receipt from State agencies. The same online system be used to generate the acknowledgement slip which will be duly signed and stamped by competent authority not below the rank of Director (Agriculture)/Mission Director (NFSNM), 2) Further, along with the copies of acknowledgement receipts from the districts, an original consolidated acknowledgement with the approval of Principal Secretary (Agri,)/Secretary (Agri,) of the respective states in the following format may be furnished to this Department. Name of the No. ol minikits Supply No. ol bio-tertilirer No. of leaflets Sl. No. Crop Variety Lot No. District supplied Date packets supplied supplied Note: Reimbursement of claims will be entertained as per the consolidated acknowledgement furnished by the agencies as per above table. 3) There will be POS based distribution by States in the areas/clusters already identified under Pulse mission for those who are taking pulses for the first time.",489,en,2025.0
CMP_Circulars_29102025_638973560481999250_Rabi Minikit 2025-26.pdf_003,other_docs,CMP_Circulars_29102025_638973560481999250_Rabi Minikit 2025-26.pdf,3,"3) There will be POS based distribution by States in the areas/clusters already identified under Pulse mission for those who are taking pulses for the first time. For others, the distribution at 50 % subsidy will be undertaken for new varieties as provided in mission.4) A farmer who has availed a minikit under this component will be ineligible to receive another minikit for the same crop or variety for a minimum period of two years. 5) The details of farmers (Farmer lD and if Farmer lD not there then land details including Khasra No., LGD code of village) be recorded and the same shall be cross verified by DCS data to confirm that same crop was grown by the farmer to which minikits were supplied. 6) The reimbursement claim by State Seed Agencies/Central Seed Agencies for minikit distribution should be submitted within 6 months of supply (cut-off date), else reasons of delay be furnished. The reimbursement claims with the delay in reasons for late submission of claims will be entertained by this Department untrll 2 years from the date of administrative approval failing in which the agencies will lose their claim amount, 7) Provisions made in the portal for allocation of seed minikits and its further distribution to districts and beneficiaries/ farmers are to be uploaded in the following NFSM portal time to ti (u https//NFSM.gov.in/mis/AdmApprovalMinikit.aspx). \./-.o $d (Dr. Dilip Kr. Srivastava) Dy. Commissioner (Pulses Mission)",236,en,2025.0
EB-52-Wheat-Cultivation-in-India-Pocket-Guide.pdf_000,other_docs,EB-52-Wheat-Cultivation-in-India-Pocket-Guide.pdf,0,"Extension Bulletin : 52 Wheat Cultivation in India Pocket Guide Anuj Kumar, Randhir Singh, Gyanendra Singh, R.K. Sharma, M.S. Saharan, R.S. Chhokar, B.S. Tyagi, Sendhil, R., Ramesh Chand and Indu Sharma ICAR - Directorate of Wheat Research Karnal-132 001, Haryana Contents General information 1 Wheat production technology 2 Choice of variety 3 Agronomic practices 6 Application of micronutrients 9 Crop protection 13 New production technologies for resource conservation 21 Laser land levelling 21 Zero tillage 22 Rotary tillage 23 Bed planting 24 Seeding into loose residues 26 Turbo happy seeder 26 Rotary disc drill (RDD) 26 Harvesting, threshing and storage 27 Costs and returns 28 4 W and 1 H of wheat cultivation 30 Wheat Cultivation in India General information Wheat (Triticum aestivum L.) is the second most important cereal crop of India and plays a vital role in food and nutritional security of the country. Nearly 55 per cent of the world population depends on wheat for about 20 per cent of calories intake. It is one of the major food grains of the country and a staple food of the people of North India, where people have preference for chapatti. The diverse environmental conditions and food habits of people in India supports the cultivation of three types of wheat (bread, durum and dicoccum). Among these, bread wheat is contributing approximately 95 per cent to total production while another 04 per cent comes from durum wheat and close to one per cent from Dicoccum. Wheat crop in India is grown under six diverse agro-climatic zones (Table 1), wherein Indo- Gangetic Plains (IGPs) comprising the two zones namely; North Western Plains Zone (NWPZ) and the North Eastern Plains Zone (NEPZ) form the major wheat tract followed by the Central Zone (CZ) and the Peninsular Zone (PZ). Table 1. Classification of wheat growing zones in India Zone Region/State Area (m. ha) Northern Hills Western Himalayan regions of J&K (except 0.9 Zone (NHZ) Jammu and Kathua distt. ); H.P. (except Una and Paonta Valley); Uttaranchal (except Tarai area); Sikkim and hills of West Bengal and N.E. States 1 Wheat Cultivation in India North Punjab, Haryana, Delhi, Rajasthan (except Kota 11.5 Western and Udaipur divisions) and Western UP (except Plains Zone Jhansi division), parts of J&K (Jammu and Kathua (NWPZ) distt.) and parts of HP (Una distt. and Paonta valley) and Uttarakhand (Tarai region) North Eastern Eastern UP, Bihar, Jharkhand, Odisha, West 11.9 Plains Zone Bengal, Assam and plains of NE States (NEPZ) Central Zone Madhya Pradesh, Chhattisgarh, Gujarat, 5.0 (CZ) Kota and Udaipur divisions of Rajasthan and Jhansi division of Uttar Pradesh Peninsular Maharashtra, Karnataka, Andhra Pradesh, 1.8 Zone (PZ) Goa and plains of Tamil Nadu Southern Hills Hilly areas of Tamil Nadu and Kerala comprising 0.1 Zone (SHZ) the Nilgiri and Palni hills of southern plateau Total 31.2 This classification of zones has been based on climatic conditions, soil types and growing duration of wheat. During wheat growing season, the expected changes in climatic factors viz; precipitation/winter rains, minimum and maximum temperature, wind velocity and its direction, sunshine hours etc.",509,en,
EB-52-Wheat-Cultivation-in-India-Pocket-Guide.pdf_001,other_docs,EB-52-Wheat-Cultivation-in-India-Pocket-Guide.pdf,1,"During wheat growing season, the expected changes in climatic factors viz; precipitation/winter rains, minimum and maximum temperature, wind velocity and its direction, sunshine hours etc. need to be considered in choosing modern varieties and matching production and protection technologies that are developed specifically to mitigate the possible anticipated effects of climate change. Wheat production technology This small pocket guide has collection of required information about the choice of wheat varieties, matching agronomic practices to be followed by the farmers and also some of the common problems related to crop protection issues that could limit wheat production and productivity at regional or farm level. Besides, the wheat growers are advised to keep track of the latest technologies on wheat and adopt these to enhance wheat productivity, and reduce cost of production so as to increase profit on sustainable basis.",138,en,
