Indian_Farming_November_2025.pdf_008,other_docs,Indian_Farming_November_2025.pdf,8,"As a result, the development of climate-resilient crops has become an urgent priority to maintain productivity in vulnerable ecosystems. Recent technologies of genome editing using CRISPR/Cas are precise, accurate and efficient tools for accelerating crop improvement. With genome editing, stress-responsive genes, regulatory components, and metabolic pathways can be specifically altered without the hassles of linkage drag or drawn-out breeding cycles. Recent advancements have successfully edited genes linked to osmotic adjustment, antioxidant regulation, ion homeostasis, disease resistance and stress signalling pathways in cereals, legumes, and oilseeds, leading to improved crop plants. In addition to resilience against abiotic stresses, editing for characteristics such as nitrogen-use efficiency, photosynthetic performance, and yield stability in variable climates presents a promising pathway for sustainable agriculture. The integration of genome editing with genomic selection, speed breeding, and high-throughput phenotyping can further expedite the creation of resilient varieties. This article emphasizes significant breakthroughs, potential gene targets, and translational strategies for implementing genome editing in crop breeding initiatives. Leveraging these innovations can greatly enhance global food and nutritional security in the context of climate change. Keywords: Abiotic stress, Cas9, CRISPR, Crop improvement, Genome editing AGRICULTURAL productivity is increasingly of specific genes or regulatory elements responsible threatened by various abiotic stresses such as for stress responses, without necessarily introducing drought, salinity, extreme temperatures (heat and cold), foreign DNA. This precision reduces unintended effects and nutrient imbalances. These environmental stressors and accelerates the development of improved crop significantly affect plant growth, development, and varieties. yield, posing a major challenge to global food security The most prominent genome editing tool, CRISPR- especially in the face of climate change and a growing Cas9, along with other systems such as TALENs world population. Although, conventional breeding (Transcription Activator-Like Effector Nucleases) and methods have been employed for decades to develop ZFNs (Zinc Finger Nucleases), has been successfully stress-tolerant crop varieties, but the major limiting applied to a wide range of crops including rice, wheat, factor is the time, as to develop improved varieties maize, tomato, and soybean. These technologies are takes years. being used to knock out negative regulators, enhance In recent years, genome editing technologies have expression of stress-responsive genes, or even introduce emerged as revolutionary tools for precise and targeted beneficial alleles for improved stress tolerance. In genetic modifications, offering new avenues for addition to providing greater control over plant traits, enhancing crop resilience to abiotic stress conditions. genome editing offers the possibility of multiplex gene Unlike conventional breeding or transgenic approaches, editing-modifying multiple genes simultaneously to genome editing allows for the direct manipulation address complex stress responses. Indian Farming 9 November 2025 As a result, genome editing holds tremendous promise Recent innovations include epigenome editing, RNA not only for improving abiotic stress tolerance but also editing, synthetic biology integration, and multiplex for contributing to sustainable agriculture, reducing the editing, enabling simultaneous modification of multiple need for chemical inputs, and supporting adaptation genes for complex trait improvement. Together, these to changing environmental conditions.",490,en,2025.0
