rful tools requires interdisciplinary enable precise gene modifications without DSBs, research, robust biosafety frameworks, and transparent reducing the risk of off-target effects and unintended public engagement. mutations. CRISPR-Cas12a has a unique PAM recognition and staggered cuts, making it suitable for *Corresponding author email: anitadgr13@gmail.com targeting inaccessible genomic regions and multiplex TEXTBOOK ON CHEESE TECHNOLOGY “Textbook on Cheese Technology” is an excellent compilation of the basics of cheese technology, incorporating the advances that are taking place in the modern cheese industry in order to tackle issues such as cheese manufacturing, quality, mechanization, safety, cost, environment etc. This textbook is written with a view to cater to the evolving needs of the rapidly growing cheese industry and highly suited for the Dairy and Food Science Colleges of the globe. TECHNICAL ASPECTS Pages: v + 369; Price: ` 600.00, US$ 90.00; Postage: `50 ISBN No. : 978-81-7164-273-1 For obtaining copies, please contact: SCAN QR Code to Purchase Online Business Unit ICAR-Directorate of Knowledge Management in Agriculture Krishi Anusandhan Bhawan – I, Pusa, New Delhi 110012 Tel: 011-25843657; email: businessuniticar@gmail.com website: www.icar.org.in 12 Indian Farming November 2025 Indian Farming 75 (11): 13-15; November 2025 Nature-based solutions with ecosystem services gains for managing saline waterlogged landscapes in semi-arid regions R. Banyal* and Varun Saini ICAR-Central Soil Salinity Research Institute, Karnal, Haryana 132 001 Soil salinity and waterlogging are major land degradation challenges, particularly in arid and semi-arid regions, severely impacting agricultural productivity and ecological stability.",497,en,2025.0
Indian_Farming_November_2025.pdf_013,other_docs,Indian_Farming_November_2025.pdf,13,"In India, approximately 8.5 million hectares are affected, largely due to poorly managed irrigation systems lacking proper drainage. Bio-drainage, using deep-rooted trees to remove excess water through transpiration emerges as a sustainable, low-cost, and eco-friendly alternative to traditional drainage methods. Tree-based bio-remediation models, particularly those involving Eucalyptus tereticornis, Prosopis juliflora, Acacia nilotica, and Tamarix aphylla, have been successfully implemented across varying degrees of salinity and waterlogging. These models provide multiple ecosystem services: provisioning (fuelwood, timber, fodder), regulating (carbon sequestration, groundwater control, climate moderation), supporting (biodiversity enhancement, soil health), and cultural (aesthetic and recreational value). Eucalyptus-based models in moderately affected areas demonstrated the highest biomass yield, carbon sequestration, and microclimate regulation. Despite these benefits, cultural services and social values remain under-researched. For broader adoption, future strategies should emphasize ecosystem service valuation, community participation, and location-specific species selection to ensure long-term sustainability and resilience of saline waterlogged landscapes. Keywords: Agroforestry, Ecological restoration, Groundwater management, Soil salinity, Waterlogging SOIL salinity and waterlogging are major forms strategies, including agroforestry, have shown promise of land degradation that threaten agricultural in managing saline waterlogged areas by providing productivity and ecological stability, particularly in arid both reclamation and a range of ecosystem services. and semi-arid regions. Globally, about 33% of irrigated land, nearly 1.0 billion hectares is affected by these twin Bio-drainage as a solution for saline waterlogged areas issues. In India, around 8.5 million hectares of lands Bio-drainage is the process by which deep-rooted are impacted, including 5.5 million ha with combined plants use their bio-energy to absorb excess soil water salinity and waterlogging problems, approximately and release it into the atmosphere through transpiration. 2.3 million ha each within and outside canal command While primarily explored for water table control, bio- areas. Primary cause is the introduction of irrigation drainage also offers both curative and preventive systems without proper drainage. States such as applications. However, its economic viability and the Haryana, Maharashtra, Gujarat, Odisha, Uttar Pradesh, broader benefits, including tangible and ecosystem Punjab, West Bengal, Bihar, Andhra Pradesh, Tamil services, remain largely unexamined in current literature. Nadu, Rajasthan, and Kerala are significantly affected. Waterlogging severity is defined by groundwater depth Tree based bio-remediation models potentially waterlogged (≤3 m), waterlogged (≤1.5 m), Tree-based models using Eucalyptus tereticornis, and severely waterlogged (0–30 cm). Traditional Prosopis juliflora, Acacia nilotica, and Tamarix aphylla were drainage methods, though effective, are capital- adopted across saline waterlogged areas, with species intensive and raise environmental concerns. In contrast, distribution varying by salinity severity. Eucalyptus bio-drainage offers a cost-effective, low-input, and thrived in less to moderately affected areas, while Prosopis, eco-friendly solution. Nature-based bio-remediation Acacia, and Tamarix were suited to severely impacted Indian Farming 13 November 2025 Eucalyptus in block model areas. In less affected regions, boundary plantations Provisioning services: Plantations in saline (1.5–2.0 m spacing) were common, transitioning to block waterlogged areas provide vital services such as food, plantations (1.0 × 3.0 m) in moderately affected areas. fodder, fuel, fibre, and timber. Eucalyptus tereticornis Severely affected zones lacke