ultivars. A comprehensive understanding of morphological, physiological, and molecular mechanisms underlying salt tolerance is crucial for this purpose. Strategies such as exploiting existing germplasm, adopting improved breeding techniques, implementing effective agronomic practices, and applying advanced technologies like genome editing hold great potential for enhancing salt tolerance in mustard. Integration of conventional breeding with modern genomic tools and genome-editing approaches will facilitate the identification and functional validation of key candidate genes and regulatory networks, thereby accelerating the development of resilient mustard cultivars for saline environments. Keywords: Crop improvement, Genome editing, Indian mustard, Salinity stress, Salt tolerance mechanism INDIAN mustard (Brassica juncea) is one of the major ultimately reducing seed yield and oil quality. To oilseed crops of India, valued for its high demand in address this issue, salt-tolerant varieties such as CS60 edible oil production. It offers significant nutritional and CS58 have been developed by ICAR-Central Soil and economic benefits and remains a preferred cooking Salinity Research Institute (ICAR-CSSRI), Karnal. These oil in eastern India, including the states of Bihar, West genotypes have demonstrated superior seed and oil Bengal, Odisha, Jharkhand, as well as Rajasthan, Punjab, yields compared to other widely cultivated varieties like and Madhya Pradesh. Edible oils derived from Indian CS54, Kranti, and Giriraj under saline conditions. mustard contribute approximately 27.8% to the country’s Given the escalating problem of soil salinization, total oilseed economy.",484,en,2025.0
