Indian_Farming_November_2025.pdf_029,other_docs,Indian_Farming_November_2025.pdf,29,"Moreover, roles in maintaining intracellular ion homeostasis salinity negatively affects photosynthetic efficiency by and nutrient balance under saline conditions. reducing chlorophyll content and impairing enzyme Harnessing these adaptive traits through systematic activities associated with photosynthesis, thereby germplasm evaluation and targeted breeding strategies severely limiting plant growth and yield. holds promise for developing high-yielding, salt-tolerant Oil quality in mustard is also compromised under mustard cultivars suitable for saline and marginal agro- saline conditions, as salt stress alters the fatty acid ecosystems. composition of the oil, diminishing both its commercial value and nutritional properties. Interestingly, B. juncea has demonstrated potential for phytoremediation of metal-contaminated saline soils, garnering attention as a candidate for sustainable agriculture and soil restoration strategies. Given these challenges, it is crucial to develop salt-tolerant mustard genotypes and systematically explore existing germplasm resources to identify and harness naturally occurring salinity tolerance traits for breeding programmes and crop improvement initiatives. Mustard’s natural salinity tolerance mechanisms Mustard exhibits low to moderate salt tolerance, attributed to several inherent adaptive mechanisms. Natural genetic variation for salinity tolerance exists within the extensive germplasm collections of mustard, offering valuable resources for crop improvement. It is essential to identify and select elite germplasm with superior adaptability to varying climatic and saline conditions through conventional breeding approaches. In this context, ICAR-Central Soil Salinity Research Institute (CSSRI), Karnal has reported several mustard genotypes exhibiting moderate to high salinity tolerance with minimal yield compromise under salt-affected environments. Several physiological, biochemical, and molecular traits contribute to the ability of mustard plants to withstand soil salinity: • Deep root system: Certain mustard germplasm possesses deeper root architectures, enabling plants to access water from subsoil layers while avoiding the detrimental effects of surface salt accumulation. • Osmotic adjustment: To mitigate osmotic stress, Variation in the root length in mustard germplasm 24 Indian Farming November 2025 Strategies for enhancing salinity tolerance in mustard AFLP, SSR, and SNP, which reveal polymorphism To reduce the nation’s dependence on edible and are closely associated with genes or oil imports and safeguard the national economy, quantitative trait loci (QTLs) governing salinity it is essential to achieve self-sufficiency in oilseed tolerance. Marker-assisted selection allows for the production, particularly mustard. In light of increasing precise identification of plants carrying desirable soil salinization and unpredictable climatic conditions, alleles, bypassing the need for time-consuming the development of salt-tolerant, high-yielding mustard and labour-intensive phenotypic screening under varieties has become a priority. Several strategies can saline conditions. Furthermore, marker-assisted be employed to enhance salinity tolerance in mustard backcross breeding (MABC) can be applied to crops: introgress identified salt tolerance QTLs into • Conventional breeding: Significant genetic high-yielding, salt-sensitive genetic backgrounds, variability for salinity tolerance exists within local thereby combining salinity resilience with desirable landraces, wild relatives, and traditional mustard agronomic traits. cultivars. Systematic identification and selection • Genome editing using CRISPR/Cas technology: of elite germplasm under saline and adverse Genome editing offers a precise and efficient climatic conditions can lead to the discovery of approach to enhance stress tolerance traits in naturally tolerant li