Indian_Farming_November_2025.pdf_042,other_docs,Indian_Farming_November_2025.pdf,42,"One of the major abiotic factors limiting plant growth and development is soil salinity. High salts concentrations in soil and water interfere plant physiological and metabolic processes, ultimately resulting in reduced growth, quality, and yield. In recent years, the application of plant growth regulators (Exogenous application or seed priming) can enhance plant tolerance to stress by modifying ionic and redox homeostasis, enhancing antioxidant activity, and affecting stress-responsive gene expression. Growth regulators such as abscisic acid, salicylic acid, jasmonic acid, gibberellins, cytokinins, and ethylene play an important role in reducing the harmful effects of salinity on plant growth and yield. Keywords: Abscisic acid, Cytokinins, Growth regulators, Gibberellins, Salinity, Salicylic acid SOIL salinity is a significant abiotic factor that impacts about 50% of arable land would be damaged by salt. crop plants and restricts global production.In India, Salinization can be occurred by two ways: primary and around 6.73 million hectares of land are impacted by secondary. Primary salinization happens spontaneously soil salinity, constituting roughly 2.1% of the nation's as a result of wind and rain-induced sea salt deposits entire geographical area Gujarat, Uttar Pradesh and or rock weathering. In arid regions, excessive irrigation Maharashtra shares more than 60% salt affected area often leads to secondary salinization, wherein salts on the country. Estimates indicate that approximately accumulate due to inadequate drainage and poor water 10% of land becomes salinized annually, and by 2050, management, compelling farmers to leave affected farms and intensifying land degradation. It is becoming major concern of 32.9% irrigated agriculture. 30 Effects of salinity stress on plants 25 Salt stress has detrimental 20.3% 20 effect of the plants. Salt stress resulted in physiological effects, 15 biochemical effects, osmotic 9.0% stress and ion imbalance. Due to 6.5% 5.4% 5.5% stomatal closure, which restricts 4.1% 3.4% 2.3% 2.2% 2.2% 2.2% 2.1% CO uptake and chlorophyll 1.1% 0.3% 0.3% 2 G U uj tt a a ra r t Pra d M e a s h h aras W ht e r s a t Benga R l ajast T h a a m n il N dh a d ra u Pra desh b e a x l r t e p e a o r k s s d u o r m e w , e n ta s b a d l o u t l r ic i s n t g r p es a e s t x h t w e d n a r d a y e s s d t i c a a s n l a l l d y t An photosynthesis.",433,en,2025.0
Indian_Farming_November_2025.pdf_043,other_docs,Indian_Farming_November_2025.pdf,43,"In plant tissues, States hazardous levels of sodium (Na+) Share (%) of Salt affected soils in India and chloride (Cl−) ions build up, 36 Indian Farming November 2025 )%( aerA lioS detceffA tlaS fo egatnecreP Salt-wise Percentage of Salt Affected Soils in India 10 5 0 haryana Bihar Punj K ab arnataka M O a d d h m is y a h a n a P & r a N d ic e o s b h ar Islan J d a s m m Ke u r & al a Kash mir And Stunt growth and poor germination of wheat under saline condition functions including protein synthesis and enzyme activation are impacted by this ionic imbalance. Salt stress modifies biochemical pathways, which results in osmolyte buildup, reactive oxygen species (ROS) generation, and modifications to metabolic processes. Role of specific growth regulators in mitigating salinity stress To reduce the detrimental effects of salt stress on plant growth and yield, two distinct strategies can be adopted. The first strategy involves using molecular or traditional breeding to create cultivars that are resistant to salt. The second strategy pertains to agronomical methods and involves applying different plant growth regulators (PGRs). Synthetic or naturally occurring organic chemicals that affect higher plant biological processes at very low concentrations are known as plant growth regulators (PGRs). PGRs are widely affordable and easy to apply to crops, and they all have the same function of controlling intrinsic hormone levels in plants by modifying signaling Effect of salinity stress on crop plants within different hormone transduction pathways. The resulting in cellular toxicity and preventing enzymatic effectiveness of PGPRs depends on the interaction activity. Ion imbalance, osmotic stress, and oxidative of host plant with soil environment. Certain PGRs damage are the causes of salt toxicity. Increased salinity have been demonstrated to improve plants' ability causes an accumulation of N