Indian_Farming_November_2025.pdf_046,other_docs,Indian_Farming_November_2025.pdf,46,"effects; multiple sprays at critical phenological stages such as tillering and flowering can effectively alleviate *Corresponding author email: poojagupta.agri@gmail.com salt-induced damage. In salt-affected irrigated systems, Plant trees, Conserve water, Protect environment. Indian Farming 39 November 2025 Indian Farming 75 (11): 40-43; November 2025 Combined effects of heavy metal toxicity and salinity on wheat: Challenges and mitigation strategies Deodate Jose George1, Vanita Pandey1, Rinki1*, Mamrutha H M1, Mahendra Firoda1, Preety Rani1, Zeenat Wadhwa1, Yogesh Kumar1, Mukesh Kumar Singh2, Gopalareddy K3 and Vanishree Girimalla3 1ICAR-Indian Institute of Wheat and Barley Research, Karnal, Haryana 132 001 2ICAR-Indian Agricultural Research Institute, Research Station, Karnal, Haryana 132 001 3ICAR-Indian Institute of Seed Science, Research Station, Gandhi Krishi Vigyana Kendra Campus, Bengaluru, Karnataka 560 065 Wheat (Triticum aestivum), a staple crop nourish nearly 40% of the global population, is increasingly threatened by soil salinity and heavy metal contamination. These stresses, often occurring together, impair photosynthesis, disrupt nutrient uptake, and enhance oxidative damage, leading to reduced yield and grain quality. Heavy metals such as lead (Pb), cadmium (Cd), arsenic (As), and chromium (Cr) accumulate in plant tissues and pose serious food safety concerns. Salinity further aggravates the problem by inducing osmotic stress. This article summarizes the combined effects of salinity and heavy metal toxicity on wheat growth and phenology, and outlines detoxification mechanisms including sequestration, chelation, and antioxidant defense. Sustainable mitigation strategies, such as plant growth-promoting rhizobacteria (PGPR), phytoremediation, bioremediation, organic amendments, and salt/metal-tolerant varieties offer effective solutions. Integrating agronomic, biological, and molecular approaches is essential to sustain wheat productivity and ensure food security under these dual stresses. Keywords: Bioremediation, Detoxification, Heavy metal, Plant growth-promoting rhizobacteria, Salinity stress, Wheat WHEAT (Triticum spp. ), a staple cereal crop from in plant tissues, disrupt plant growth and metabolic the Poaceae family, provides food and nutrition functions, and pose significant risks to human and to nearly 40% of the global population and contributes animal health via the food chain. Soil salinity, often approximately 20% of the world's daily calorie and co-occurring with HM contamination, is another major protein intake. It is vital to global food security, especially constraint to crop productivity, particularly in arid and in developing nations, and supports the livelihoods of semi-arid regions. According to FAO (2022), salinity over 80 million smallholder farmers. India ranks second affects up to 50% of irrigated lands in some regions and globally in wheat production, contributing about 11.9% is expected to worsen due to climate change. Salt stress of global output and cultivating approximately 12% of impairs plant functions by causing ionic and osmotic the total wheat-growing area. stress, oxidative damage, membrane disruption, and Among the growing challenges in wheat cultivation, metabolic imbalances—ultimately reducing growth and heavy metal (HM) contamination and soil salinity yield. Combined exposure to heavy metals and salinity pose serious threats to sustainable crop production. exacerbates the generation of reactive oxygen species Heavy metals such as lead (Pb), cadmium (Cd), arsenic (ROS), leading to oxidative stress, DNA damage, protein (As), copper (Cu), and nickel (Ni) are introduced into denaturation, and lipid peroxidation.",503,en,2025.0
