R-Central Soil Salinity Research Institute, Karnal, Haryana 132 001 Waterlogged saline soils are severely affecting crop production in irrigated areas of the country and appropriate technologies need to be adopted for their reclamation. Subsurface drainage (SSD) is proven technology for reclamation of such soils and about 1,10,000 ha area has been reclaimed in India using this technology. However, very high installation cost, non-availability of adequate trencher machines and need of region/soil specific design requirements has resulted in its adoption at very slow pace. The brief information of SSD technology covering its benefits, components, installation, and a few case studies of its performance in different parts of the country is presented in this article. Keywords: Crop productivity, Food security, Sub-surface drainage technology, Waterlogged saline soils LAND degradation poses significant threat for the waterlogging and soil salinity problems. Waterlogged sustaining agricultural production in the country saline soils occur on about 2 million hectares area in arid and climate change is further aggravating its impact and semi-arid alluvial north western states of India and on crop production. In the past, irrigation has played more than 1 million hectares each in coastal and black an important in Indian agriculture for safeguarding cotton soil (Vertisol) regions of the country. However, in the nation’s food security by ensuring availability of India, area covered under subsurface drainage system adequate irrigation water to high yielding varieties is only about 80,000 ha; and the faster pace of adoption through extensive canal network.",497,en,2025.0
