approaches, will enable better soil management and land use planning, contributing to sustainable agricultural SUMMARY practices and land conservation efforts. Digital Soil Mapping (DSM) represents the future of soil salinity diagnostics. By integrating advanced *Corresponding author email: amu8805@gmail.com remote sensing, geospatial technologies, and machine E-mail: businessuniticar@gmail.com Importance of Millets and Improved Technologies “Importance of Millets and Improved Technologies” This book is an excellent compilation of knowledge of importance of Millets and improved production technologies which is essential to create awareness among the end users for their promotion. Millets are being utilized for various purposes such as food, feed, fodder and more recently, as bio-fuel. For this information, importance of different millet crops in our livelihood and their technological advancement has been compiled in this monogram. This will be of immense value to the researchers, academicians, end-users and policymakers alike to promote millets farming. TECHNICAL ASPECTS Pages: v + 183; Price: ` 700.00, US$ 90.00; Postage: `50 ISBN No.",421,en,2025.0
Indian_Farming_November_2025.pdf_071,other_docs,Indian_Farming_November_2025.pdf,71,"This will be of immense value to the researchers, academicians, end-users and policymakers alike to promote millets farming. TECHNICAL ASPECTS Pages: v + 183; Price: ` 700.00, US$ 90.00; Postage: `50 ISBN No. : 978-81-7164-272-4 SCAN QR Code to For obtaining copies, please contact: Purchase Online Business Unit ICAR- Directorate of Knowledge Management in Agriculture Krishi Anusandhan Bhawan – I, Pusa, New Delhi 110012 Tel: 011-25843657; email: businessuniticar@gmail.com website: www.icar.org.in 60 Indian Farming November 2025 Indian Farming 75 (11): 61-63; November 2025 Waste wool technology in arid horticulture: Promoting sustainability and circular economy Anita Meena*, M. K. Jatav, Manpreet Kaur and R. P. Meena ICAR-Central Institute for Arid Horticulture, Bikaner, Rajasthan 334 006 Arid and semi-arid regions face significant agricultural limitations due to environmental and soil-related challenges such as erratic rainfall, poor soil fertility, and extreme temperatures. These constraints hinder horticultural productivity, which relies on stable soil moisture and nutrient availability. To promote sustainability and productivity in these regions, attention has shifted toward using locally available resources. One such resource is waste wool, a by-product of sheep rearing is an important livelihood in arid zones. Though unsuitable for textiles, waste wool is rich in organic carbon and nutrients, making it an effective soil amendment. When applied to horticultural systems, it improves soil structure by increasing porosity, moisture retention, and organic content. This not only enhances plant growth in harsh climates but also promotes ecological sustainability. Moreover, utilizing waste wool reduces agricultural input costs, turning a low-value by-product into a valuable resource. This practice strengthens the resilience of arid-zone horticulture and supports rural economies by aligning with circular economy principles. Overall, use of waste wool offers a practical and sustainable solution to improve soil health, increase productivity, and support environmental stewardship in arid and semi-arid regions. Keywords: Ecological, Rural economies, Soil health A substantial quantity of organic waste and by- practices that rely heavily on chemical inputs and non- products is generated globally each year, much renewable resources. The urgency of such innovations of which holds significant potential for reuse within has been amplified by the increasing challenges facing agricultural and horticultural systems. Among these global agriculture. Climate change has intensified the by-products, waste generated by the wool industry frequency of droughts, heatwaves, and erratic rainfall, represents an underutilized resource with considerable particularly in arid and semi-arid regions where value for sustainable farming. The global wool industry water scarcity severely constraints crop production. produces millions of tonnes of wool annually, but a large Additionally, continuous land use, overreliance on fraction of this yield is categorized as waste, as well as chemical fertilizers, and unsustainable cultivation due to fiber irregularities or poor quality unsuitable for practices have accelerated soil degradation, reducing textile applications. Traditionally, much of this wool fertility and organic matter content while increasing waste has been discarded in landfills or incinerated, susceptibility to erosion and salinization. These leading not only to environmental degradation but also challenges necessitate the adoption of ecologically sound to a significant loss of potential organic resources.",500,en,2025.0
