Advisory on  Blast disease (Magnaporthe oryzae) on rice crop.pdf_000,ppqs_advisories,Advisory on  Blast disease (Magnaporthe oryzae) on rice crop.pdf,0,"Phone:0129-2413985 E-mail:-ppa@nic.in F.No 3-6/2022-23llPl'UAdvisorv sfl-<I(rcF, V Government of India TR q{ Rqr{ 6ensr {a.ltrt / Ministry of Agriculture & Farmers Welfare TR qd' fur{ +-eqrur RqFr I Department of Agriculture & Farmers Welfare aqqfr€'(tilrr, ftrfrEq{ffitqntrq/ Directorate dPlant Protection, Quarantine & Storage q{gs4, wfter*r<1qft{rsrD - 121 001/ NH-IV, Faridabad (Haryana) - l2l 001 Dated;22.07.2022 To The Directors of Agriculture UTs All States/ r Sub: Advisory on blast disease (Magnaporthe oryzae) on rice crop -reg. Sir, I have the honor to bring to your notice that Paddy blast is an important disease which is responsible for significant yield losses in rice crop. This disease is caused by the fungus Magnaporthe oryzae which can infect rice crop at all growth stages and all aerial parts of the plant leaves, neck, nodes and seeds. Leaf and neck infections are more severe and infection during seedling stage may cause severe damage to the crop. Rice blast is favored by low temperatures (22-280C1, high relative humidity (>95%), dew deposits, leaf wetness for more than 10 hours, application ofhigh nitrogen and aerobic soils. Keeping in view the prevailing favourable weather conditions for the disease development the rice crop. should be monitored regularly for disease development and adopt Integrated Disease Management techniques. AESA based IPM Package of practices of rice and disease specific package of practices of rice blast are available at DPPQ&S website in the link httlr://ppqs.gov.irVipm-packaees. Important IPM practices are listed below . Keep the field clean, remove infected seeds, panicles, and plant debris after harvest. o Crop rotation with non host crops, it provides a mechanism that separates viable spores in crop residue from the newly emerging seedlings o Use high quality disease free certified seeds. Infected seeds may serve as inoculum forfurther spread and development. o Treat the seeds with Pseudomonas fluorescence 0.5%o WP (TNAU Strain Accession No. ITCC BE 0005) 109/I(9 of seed or Pseudomondsfluorescens 1.5%o WP (BIL-331 Accession No. MTCC5866) 5gA(g of seed or Pseudomonas fluorescens l.5Yo LF (MTCC no. 5671, Strain designation Pf-l) 4.5 mVKg of seed gives protection soil bome and seed borne diseases. . Dip the seedlings in with Pseudomonas fluorescence Sgllit of water for 20 minutes before transplanting. Pseudomonas stppresses the diseases and also acts as plant growth promotion and reduces the severity of many diseases. o Broadcast I kg Pseudomonas fluorescens 0.5Yo WP (TNAU Strain Accession No. ITCC BE 0005) by mixing with 2.5 kg organic manure in one ha area o Removal and destruction (burn) of diseased plant parts o Maintaining a proper water level for the paddy to grow. Rice blast is known to be more severe in fields or parts of fields in which the water in paddy falls below recommended levels. ..............To be continued 2- Foliar spray with Pseudomonas fluorescence (TNAU Strain Accession No. ITCC BE 0005) 5gllit of water @ Ikglha at an interval of 15-20 days after transplanting or Pseudomonas fluorescens 1.s%LF (MTCC no. 567I, Strain designation Pf-l) @ 6lt/ha could be applied. Fungicides must be used as per label claim approval by the Registration Committee. Detailed information is available at directorate website.",512,en,2022.0
Advisory on  Blast disease (Magnaporthe oryzae) on rice crop.pdf_001,ppqs_advisories,Advisory on  Blast disease (Magnaporthe oryzae) on rice crop.pdf,1,"567I, Strain designation Pf-l) @ 6lt/ha could be applied. Fungicides must be used as per label claim approval by the Registration Committee. Detailed information is available at directorate website. http://ppqs.gov.in/divisions/cib- rclmajor-uses-of-pesticides. Further the list of RC approved fungicides against rice blast are attached as Annexure-I. Further the following strategies should be adopted for spreading awareness among farmers o Regular monitoring of disease incidence is very essential to take necessary proactive measrres. Accurate and timely forecasting of the disease incidence would support in planning effective mitigation. o Awareness among farming community through newspapers, printing and electronic media, KisanGosthi, leaflet and pamphlets. o The concemed officials atthe district/ division/ block level mav be advised to take the suitable plant protection measures immediately The detailed information on disease incidence may also be sent to the directorate through email ppa@nic.in or appa.ipm-dppqs@gov.in urgently to apprise the same to Department of Agriculture & Farmers'Welfare. Yours faithfully Encl: as above Copy for information: 1. PPS to Joint Secretary@P), DA & FW, Krishi Bhawan, New Delhi- I 10001 2. PPS to PPA for information J. Officer In-charges, all CIPMCs with directions to conduct regular survey in the area to ensure and necessary follow-up with the state authorities.",200,en,2022.0
