cide dealcrs and farmers should be organizeO 6y tfr""c SAUs/ ICAR Institutes/ CIPfi4Cs/ KVKs to update their technical knowiedge and skill regarding management of Aphid'- 6. Insccticides rnust ue useo u, p., labJl claim appioved . by the central Insecticides Board and ltegistration Committee. Detailed information is available at http://cibrc'nic'in/mJp'htm' I lowcver, list of recommendecl pesticides for aphid is attached' (Annexure-I) 7. IpM packagc of practiccs for wheat need to be adopted. 'l'his may be downloaded from web- link hgpJlplgleov.in/ipmpakpra revised.htm or http://farmer.sov.inllPM packageof Practices. htm In view of the above, it is advised to undertake monitoring of wheat crop on regular basis and if any incidence of aphid is reported by the farmers, extension functionaries and even through the newspapers and/or other sources; survey of the affected area may be conducted immediately and the concerned officials at the Division/DistrictiBlgg$ may be advised to take the suitable plant protection measures immediately. The detailed information may also be sent to this Directorate through Fax (Nos. 0129-2412125 or 011,23384182) or e- mail: ppa@nic.in or appaipm-ppqs@sov.in urgently to apprise the Department of Agriculture, Cooperation & Farmers Welfare. Yours faithfully, at illlIlilll z (D.D.K. Sharma) Plant Protection Adviser Copy to: 1. Additional Secretary GP), DAC&FW, MOA&FW, KrishiBhawan, New Delhi. . ' 2. Joint Secretary (PP), DAC&FW, MOA&FW, Krishi Bhawan, New Delhi, 3. PS to PPA 4. Officer I/C of CIPMC, Gorakhpur, Agra and Lucknow is advised to. monitor intensively and remain in touch with the State Government for follow up action.",496,en,
