Advisory on Fall Army Worm (FAW) (spodoptera frugiperda) on Maize Crop.pdf_000,ppqs_advisories,Advisory on Fall Army Worm (FAW) (spodoptera frugiperda) on Maize Crop.pdf,0,"ffi ffi *T Phone:0129-2413985 E-mai1:-ppa@nic.in F.No 3-6l2022-23llPM/Advisorv sfT{ftfg$fV Government of India 5R qq Agrt +-""qrur riTrcfi' / Ministry of Agriculture & Farmers Welfare TRq*ftqm+-e+rrrftrrm i Department of Agriculture & Farmers Welfare a6qft6qq, trrfrrrqt""idT{ff?eTrd:I / Directorate of Plant Protection, euarantine & Storage \r{\rE4, -rftErqret€RqltIlT) - 121 001/NH-IV, Faridabad (Haryana) _ 121 001 Dated: 12.07.2024 To, The Director of Agriculture Maharashtra Sub: Advisory on Fall Army worm (FAw) spodoptera frugiperda on Maize - regarding. Sir, It is to bring to lour notice that Fall Armyworm (Spodoptera frugiperda) is a polyphagous economically important insect pest. Its incidence is reported above ETL on maize in Nagpur and Amravati districts in 3 hectare area and Village Khuntewadi and Pimpalgaon of Nashik district in 310 hectare area of Maharashtra. Keeping in view the favourable climatic conditions, availability of maize crop throughout country and previous year observations there is possibility that the pest population may increase therefore the following strategies are suggested well in advance to prevent the pest attack. o Follow clean cultivation i.e, removal of altemate hosts helps to break down life cycle of FAW and hand picking and destruction of egg masses and larvae in mass by crushing or by immersing in water o Pre sowing and summer ploughing will expose FAw pupae to predators. . Follow intercropping of maize with suitable pulse crops such as pigeon pea, black gram/green gram o Erection of bird perches @ I}laueduring early stage of the crop (up to 30 days) as birds acts as good predator on lepidopteran larvae o Sowing of 3-4 rows of trap crops (e.g. Napier) around maize field and spraywith 5% NSKE or Azadirachtin 1500 ppm as soon as the trap crop shows symptom of FAW damage. o Install pheromone traps @ 1S/acre in field formass trapping and destruction of male moths ; -2- Eggs parasitoids such as Trichogramma pretiosum and Telemonus remus can be released for destroying the egg stage of FAW @ 50000 per acre at weekly intervals (avoid spraying chemical pesticides for few.days during the time of release in the field) Pesticides should be used as per label claim approved by the Registration Committee. A list of recommended Biopesticides for Fall armywonn in maize is available on website link defa icides in bio i nsecticides as on 31.03.2024.pdf and recommended pesticides is available on website link https://ppqs.gov.inlsites/default/files/major_uses_of pesticides insecticides as on 31 .03.2024.pdf Further the following strategies should be adopted for spreading awareness among farmers o Mobilization of extension functionaries at grass route level fbr regular local monitoring of pest status through NPSS and coordinate with RCIPMC, Nagpur for timely.advisory. o Regular meetings of State Pest Surveillance and- advisory unit and District pest Surveillance and Advisory Unit may be conducted o Awareness among farming community through newspapers, printing and electronic media, Kisan Gosthi, leaflet and pamphlets. o The concemed officials at the district/ division/ block level may be advised to take the suitable plant protection measures.",486,en,2024.0
Advisory on Fall Army Worm (FAW) (spodoptera frugiperda) on Maize Crop.pdf_001,ppqs_advisories,Advisory on Fall Army Worm (FAW) (spodoptera frugiperda) on Maize Crop.pdf,1,"o The concemed officials at the district/ division/ block level may be advised to take the suitable plant protection measures. The detailed information on pest incidence may also be sent to the directorate through email to ppa@nic.in or appa.ipm-dppqs@gov.in urgently to apprise the same to Depirtment of Agriculture& Farmers' Welfare. MoA&FW. Yours faithfully (Dr.J. Plant Protection Copy for information: ' 1 2 . . PPS to Additional Secrerary (pp), DAC & Fw, Krishi Bhawan, New Delhi- I 10001 The Directors of Agriculture of oiher maize growing states with the request to keep close watch on pest build up & take appropriate measures as indicated above 3. Officer In-charges, all CIPMCs of maize growing states with directions to conduct regular survey in the area using NPSS and issue timely advisory in coordination with local SAU & ICAR institutes also to.ensure and necessary follow-up with the state authorities. p",147,en,2024.0
Advisory on Fall Army Worm (FAW) Spodoptera frugiperda on Maize crop.pdf_000,ppqs_advisories,Advisory on Fall Army Worm (FAW) Spodoptera frugiperda on Maize crop.pdf,0,"Phone: 0129-2413985 E-mail:-ppa@nic.in 6i' s6'""tq€8? 'd F.No 3-6l2022-23lrpNvAdvisorv STrtiRICFrV Government of Ind,a TRqd'ftqrto-qrurriare-q / Ministry of Agriculture & Farmers Welfare TR qd'fr{|T+eqrurRrrnr I Department of Agriculture & Farmers Welfare +tgRdarur, {rl-fusiffi?qrrdq / Directorate of Ptant Protection, Quarantine & Storage qrgq4, .rter-dRtERsruo - 121 001/ NH-IV, Faridabad (Haryana) - 121 001 Dated: Z?- .07.2022 To The Directors of Agriculture All States/ Union Territories Sub: Advisory on Fall Army Worm (FAW) Spodoptera frugiperda on Maize - regarding. Sir, I have the honour to bring to your kind notice that the Fall Armyworm (Spodoptera frugiperda) is an invasive, polyphagous economically important insect pest could cause substantial damage especially in maize crop. The pest is native to tropical and sub-tropical regions of America. In India it spread from South India to other parts of the country. Nevertheless its damage intensity is under control by continuous efforts of DPPQ&S, FAO, ICAR and State Agricttlture/ Horticulture departments. Keeping in view the favourable climatic conditions, availability of maize crop throughout country and previous year observations there is possibility that the pest may cause extensive damage therefore the following strategies are suggested to prevent the pest attack well in advance Extensive surveys need to be initiated to map the prevalence and spread of the pest. Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO), Rome, Italy developed the FAW \4onitoring and Early Warning System (FAMEWS) mobile app can used by farmers, and extension agents to send vital data about FAW infestation levels. This , helps generate detailed and reliable information that can be used to manage FAW. This app works offline and provides farmers with advice on how to manage FAW Follow clean cultivation i.e, removal df alternate hosts helps to break down life cycle of FAW and hand picking and destruction of egg masses and larvae in mass by crushing or by immersing in water o Deep ploughing before sowing will expose FAW pupae to predators. a Timely and uniform sowing over a large area is advised. Avoid staggered sowing. o Follow intercropping of maize with suitable pulse crops such as pigeon pea, black gram/green gram Erection of bird perches @ T}lacre during early stage of the crop (up to 30 days) as birds acts as good predator on lepidopteran larvae To be continued in next page -2- Sowing of 3-4 rows of trap crops (e.g. Napier) around maize freld and spray with 5% NSKE or Azadirachtin 1500 ppm as soon as the trap crop shows symptom of FAW damage.",409,en,2022.0
Advisory on Fall Army Worm (FAW) Spodoptera frugiperda on Maize crop.pdf_001,ppqs_advisories,Advisory on Fall Army Worm (FAW) Spodoptera frugiperda on Maize crop.pdf,1,"Napier) around maize freld and spray with 5% NSKE or Azadirachtin 1500 ppm as soon as the trap crop shows symptom of FAW damage. Application of Sand + lime in 9: I ration in whorls in first thirty days of sowing soon after the observation of FAw incidence restricts larval feeding Install pheromone traps @ l5lacre in field for mass trapping and destruction of male moths Eggs parasitoids such as Trichogramma pretiosum utd Telemonus remus can be released for destroying the egg stage of FAW @ 50000 per acre at weekly intervals (avoid spraying chemical pesticides for few days during the time of release in the field) hogenic fungi such as Metarhizium anisopriae, Nomuraea rirevi, bassiana and verticilium recqni at l x 108 cFU/g @ 5 glritre whorr & repeat after 10 days if required could be used for management of the pest, Bacillus thuringiensrs ver. Kurstaki, NpV formulation @ 2 gll (or) 400 glacre could be used IPM package of practices for Maize need to be promoted. The IpM package of practices can be downloaded from o Insecticides must be used as recommended by Registration Committee of CIB&RC. The recommended list of Pesticides are available at link if major I i n n c v i i d e e w n c o e f t o h f e F a A bo W ve , i s i t re is p r o e r q te u d e s b te y d t t h o e m fa o r n m it e o r r s t , h e e x t m en a s iz io e n cr o fu p n c o t n io r n e a g r u ie la s r a ba n s d i s e a ve nd n through newspapers or other sources, survey of the affbcted fields may be conducted immediately and the concerned officials at the district/ division/ block may be advised to take the suitable plant protection measures immediately. The detailed information may also be sent to the Directorate through email ppa@nic.in or appa.ipm-ppqs@gov.in urgently to apprise the same to Department of Agriculture & Farmers welfare. Yours faithfuily Encl: as above e2l1^1zf ( PIant Protection Adviser Copy for information: 1. 2. PPS to p Jo p i A nt Secretary(pp), DA & FW, Krishi Bhawan, New Delhi_ l r000r PPS to for information 3' officer In-charges, All cIPMCs with directions to conduct regular survey in the area to ensure and necessary follow_up with the st rte authorities.",437,en,2022.0
Advisory on false Smut of rice caused by Ustiloginoideu virens.pdf_000,ppqs_advisories,Advisory on false Smut of rice caused by Ustiloginoideu virens.pdf,0,"Phone:0129-2476370 aq- +-ar{ sa=gat *t rir* F.No3 -6120 19 -20 - IPM- AdvisorY efl|f,{{fiR/ Government of India Offil-n-*<.qfrf""i?Tilq-/ Ministry of Agriculture & Farmers Welfare TR'@DepartmentofAgriculture.Cooperation&FarmersWelfare qa€qF:H{ailT, ffiq !rd' wr6 Hdqnaq, \rfl(rE 4, St-drdrd (dtrqrwy DIRECTORATEoFPLANTPROTECTION'QUARANTINE&STORAGE (HARYANA) l2l00l N.H.-lV. FAzuDABAD Dated: 1212020 To, The Director of Agdculture Directorate of Agriculture Government of Uttar Pradesh Madan Mohan MalviYa Marg Krishi Bhavan, Lucknow -22600I Subject: Advisory on false Smut of rice caused by (lstiloginoideu virens- regarding Dear Sir, This has come to the knowledge of the Directorate that False Smut in rice commonly known as Kandwa Disease of Rice was reported in Santkabir, Basti, Siddhartha Nagar, Gorakhpur, Maharajganj, Kushinagar, Devart'a and Ayodhya district of U'P in the year 2020- 21. It is imperative to conduct sensitization ploglalnme among farmers for management of false smut of Rice to avoid re-occulrence of disease in next rice growing Season' As primary source of inoculums for false smut is soil, altemate host or contaminated seed, so it is highly desirable to ensure inclusion of topics on management of false smut of rice in Famiers Field School GFS) and other farmers training plogrammes conducted by state agriculture deparlment of the ongoing season also and necessaly preparation for management of disease during ensuing rice growing season. Further, it is requested that whenever FFS and other training programmes are being conducted by the State Agriculture Department may kindly be brought to the knowledge of RCIPMC. Lucknow. Contd..l2.,. a -L- An advisory on the management of false smut is enclosed herewith for readY reference as Annexure-I. Yours faithfrrlly, Dr. Ravi Prakash Plant Protection Adviser Copy to: l. Officers I/C of CIPMCs, Lucknougo keep close watch on the irrci&rcefalse smut in rice and be in touch with the state 6ven ment for follow up action. 2. PS to JS (PP), DAC&FW, Krishi Bhawan, New Delhi for kind information' 3. IT- Cell for uploading on DPPQ&S, website. Annexure-I Advisory on the management of false smut of rice (Causal organism: Fungi- Ustiloginoidea virens) False smut of rice was reported in Santkabir, Basti, Siddhartha Nagar, Gorakhpur, Maharajganj, Kushinagar, Devaria and Ayodhya district of U.P in the year 2019-20 in significant level, therefore, has potential to cause significant damage in 2021-22 Kharif season also in the above mentioned area and may further spread in other rice growing areas of states under favorable weather conditions. The disease occur in areas with high relative humidity (>90%) and temperature ranging from 25−35 ºC. Rain, high humidity, and soils with high nitrogen content also favors disease development & wind can spread the fungal spores from plant to plant. Primary source of inoculums is soil, alternate host or contaminated seeds. Keeping in view of the above, it is advised that the all rice growing areas of Uttar Pradesh states may be intensively monitored during Rapid Roving Survey. Strong linkage among State Agriculture Department, State Agricultural University, ICAR Institutes and farmers to implement Integrated Pest Management (IPM) at village level need to be developed.",492,en,2020.0
Advisory on false Smut of rice caused by Ustiloginoideu virens.pdf_001,ppqs_advisories,Advisory on false Smut of rice caused by Ustiloginoideu virens.pdf,1,"Strong linkage among State Agriculture Department, State Agricultural University, ICAR Institutes and farmers to implement Integrated Pest Management (IPM) at village level need to be developed. The following practices of IPM strategies may be followed for management of false smut on Rice crop Symptoms False smut is visible only after panicle exertion. It can infect the plant during flowering stage. Plants infected with false smut have individual rice grain transformed into a mass of spore balls. These spore balls are initially orange, and then turn into greenish black when these mature. In most cases, only few grains in a panicle are usually infected and the rest are normal. Orange coloured spore ball Mature black spore balls Disease Cycle and Epidemiology The disease can occur in areas with high relative humidity (>90%), temperature ranging from 25−35 ºC and moderate rainfall well distributed during the period of flowering. Soil with high nitrogen content also favors disease development. Wind can spread the fungal spores from plant to plant. Nursery/seedling stage to transplanting stage  Keep the field clean, remove infected seeds, panicles, plant debris after harvest and removal of alternate host.  Crop rotation with non host crops, it provides a mechanism that separates viable spores in crop residue from the newly emerging seedlings  Use high quality disease free certified seeds. Seeds contaminated with fungal spore may serve as inoculum for further spread and development.  Early planted crop has less smut ball than the late planted crop  Hot water treatment of seeds at 520C for 10 mins  Treat the seeds with Pseudomonas fluorescence 10g/lit of water for 30 minutes or Trichoderma viridae @5-10 g/kg of seeds gives protection soil borne and seed borne diseases.  Dip the seedlings in with Pseudomonas fluorescence 5g/lit of water for 20 minutes before transplanting. Pseudomonas suppresses the diseases and also acts as plant growth promotion and reduces the severity of many diseases.  Apply neem cake @150 kg/ha Vegetative to panicle initiation stage:  Regular monitoring of disease incidence is very essential to take necessary proactive measures  Balanced use of fertilizers and micro-nutrients as per local recommendations. Reduce humidity levels through alternate wetting and drying of fields rather than permanently flooding the fields.  Foliar spray with Pseudomonas fluorescence 5g/lit of water at an interval of 15-20 days after transplanting  Spray recommended fungicides only after the observation of initial infection of the disease Flowering to harvest stage  Regular monitoring of disease incidence  At the time of harvesting diseases plants should be removed and destroyed, so that sclerotia do not fall in the field. This will reduce the primary inoculum for the next season.  Spray recommended need based fungicides only after the observation of initial infection of the disease  Foliar spray with Pseudomonas fluorescence 5g/lit of water at an interval of 15-20 days after transplanting.",477,en,2020.0
Advisory on false Smut of rice caused by Ustiloginoideu virens.pdf_002,ppqs_advisories,Advisory on false Smut of rice caused by Ustiloginoideu virens.pdf,2,This will reduce the primary inoculum for the next season.  Spray recommended need based fungicides only after the observation of initial infection of the disease  Foliar spray with Pseudomonas fluorescence 5g/lit of water at an interval of 15-20 days after transplanting.  Spray with recommended fungicides having lesser waiting period List fungicides approved against false smut of paddy Fungicides Dosage per ha Waiting period from a.i (g) Formulatio Dilution last n (g/ml)% in water application (L) to harvest (in days) Copper Hydroxide 53.8% 525 1500 500 10 Copper Hydroxide 77%WP 1000 gm 2000 gm 750 - Fluopyram 17.7% + Fluopyram1 550 g/ha 500 22 Tebuconazole 17.7%w/w 10 SC +Tebconazo le110 Picoxystrobin 7.05% + 200 1000 500 24 Propiconazole 11.7% SC Tebuconazole 50% + - 350-400 500 35 Trifloxystrobin 25% WG,132,en,2020.0
