Advisory on management of soil borne diseases of chickpea  crop.pdf_002,ppqs_advisories,Advisory on management of soil borne diseases of chickpea  crop.pdf,2,"y i r o e m i c n 1 w w o e n e t ' t whitish mycelial suanOs cai i#t;i"";' *p*eedlike sclerotia can be observed' soil i M n a th n e a g e e a m rly e n s t t a o g f e t s h e ""f s e soil-bom"" di.eur"", are mainly de o p f e th n e d s s e o d n i s p e r a e s e s s o w in i n c g h ic p k ra p c e t a lc ' e S s e e so d pro-"",i"", adoption and impor "" t f a ;; n ;; t ; ; ; i "" l i ; i ; "" ; * 1 1 - "" ; a "" "" t * ic r^ e i"" c fo id r e so n il c b e o rne <lisease in chickpea so keeping t i r n e a v t i m ew en t t h i e s c a w n rent rab * i , i "" l u ;; r 6 on ; l ; I in n t"" p gr o ut r "" t d u p , e r s t t m ,o a l n "" a i g n e ,s m u e c n ce t s te s c fu h l n m iq a u n e a s g s e h m o e u n ld t o b f e s f o o il l lo b w or e n d e before sowing of crops dir""ur"", of chickpea' Important IPM practices are listed below o coilection and destruction of diseased crop debris, stubbles, un-decomposed organic . ffit:::i;""v sowing of crop as to escape from high soil moisture (earlv sowing' co1lar rot) and drought stress (late sowing' dry root ror) a Adopt wider spacing to restrict the spread of disease from one plant to another' a Soil solarisation during hot summer reduce the primary inoculum. a Since sclerotia/chlamydospores are viable in the soil for more than one year, field having high field infestation during previous year, should be avoided for sowing of crops' Avoid sowing of chickpea in the field where wilt incidence was more in last year Use resistant/tolerant varieties of chickpea specific to these diseases. Deep sowing ofchick pea about 8- 10 centimeters deep in the light soils reduces the wilt incidence. seed treatment with bioagents like Trichoderma harzianum @ 6 gnikg seed for root rot or Trichoderma viride @ 5 g&g of seeds for wilt. Also apply bio-agents Like Trichodermd spp' or Pseudomonas in soil @ 3-5 kg/ln to reduce the soil bome inoculums. a Avoid flood inigation which favours the incidence ofdiseases like collar rot and wilt.",495,en,2023.0
Advisory on management of soil borne diseases of chickpea  crop.pdf_003,ppqs_advisories,Advisory on management of soil borne diseases of chickpea  crop.pdf,3,"Also apply bio-agents Like Trichodermd spp' or Pseudomonas in soil @ 3-5 kg/ln to reduce the soil bome inoculums. a Avoid flood inigation which favours the incidence ofdiseases like collar rot and wilt. Light inigation at the time of disease incidence under stress condition to minimize the soil temp (Dry root rot). Registration committee approved pesticides are available as seed dresser. Detailed information is on directorate's website ar link available 1.06.2023.pdf A list of recommended pesticides for soil borne diseases of chickpea t attached at Annexure-I. Further the following strategies should be adopted for spreading awareness among farmers o Mobilization of extension functionaries at grass route level for conducting of seed treatment campaign. o Awareness among farming community through newspapers, printing and electronic media, Kisan Gosthi, leaflet and pamphlets for adoption of seed treatment programme to avoid incidence of soil bome diseases. The detailed information on pest incidence may also be sent to the directorate through email to pBa@ntdn or appa.ipm-dppqs@gov.in urgently to apprise the same to Department of Agriculture& Farmers' Welfare, MoA & FW. Yours faithfully Encl: as above (Dr. J. P. Singh) Plant Protection Adviser Copy for information: i. PPS to Joint Secretary(PP), DAC & FW, Krishi Bhawan, New Delhi- I 10001 2. PPS to PPA for information 3. Officer In-charges, all CIPMCs of chickpea growing states with directions to conduct seed treatment campaign in chickpea growing areas. Annexure-I Approved registered fungicides for soil borne diseases of chickpea Chemical Disease Dosage/IIa Waiting Period(Davs) a.i (gm) Formulation Dilution in (gm/nl) Water (Liter) Tebuconazole Root rot, 0.24 910 4.0 ml/l0 Kg Seed dresser 5.4% w/w FS Wilt Kg of of seed seed Prochlonz 5.7Vo + Root rot, 0.18+ 3.0 mV10 kg Seed dresser Tebuconazole Wilt 0.045 seeds 1.4Yow/wES Tebuconazole 15% Root rot, 2.0 4.0 Sufficient NA Seed + Zineb 57%;o wilt to coat the dresser WDG seeds uniformly",308,en,2023.0
Advisory on management of Tea Mosquito Bug (Helopeltis theivora) in Tea crop.pdf_000,ppqs_advisories,Advisory on management of Tea Mosquito Bug (Helopeltis theivora) in Tea crop.pdf,0,"Phone: 0129-2413985 E-mail: -appa. ipm-ppqs@gov. in F.No 3-6l2023-24llPM/Advisorv rlT(ilTC;fi,TV Government of India TR \t{ Rffit.q s€rrTur {Trf,q / Ministry of Agriculture & Farmers Welfare TR \t{ frqr{ s-€{tqG\TrrI / Department of Agriculture & Farmers Welfare T{€qft*t(Qfq, ffiqqqTiT{fttqr|T|q/ Directorate of Plant Protection, Quarantine & Storage q{w4, .ntqr{r< r$sruru - l2l001/ NH-IV, Faridabad (Haryana) - 121 001 Dated: 25n4.2023 To, The Director of Horticulture. Tamil Nadu. The Director of Agriculture, West Bengal, Assam & Kerala. Sub: Advisory on management of Tea Mosquito Bug (Helopeltis theivora) in Tea crop -reg. Sir, This is to bring to your notice that the incidence of Helopeltis theivora (Tea Mosquito Bug) has reported above moderate intensity from Tea growing districts of Tamil Nadu state. It is a serious pest and may cause damage & eventually yield loss. Considering its status and host range which includes cashew, cinchona, cocoa, neem, cotton, cowpea, red gram, henna, apple, grapes, guava, avocado, drumstick, mango, ber, Annona reticulata, allspice, black pepper, Ailanthus, mahogany, annatto, Thespesia populnea, Solanum nigrum, Singapore cherry, ornamental Acalypha etc. and keeping in view the current crop stage and prevailing weather conditions, population of this pest is expected to build up. Therefore, Tea and other host crop should be monitored regularly for pest build up and adoption of Integrated Pest Management techniques. Agro Ecosystem Analysis (AESA) based IPM Package of practices of Tea is available at DPPQ&S website in the link http://ppqs.gov.in/ipm- packages. The Important IPM practices for management of Tea Mosquito Bug are listed below: o Regular survey and monitoring its population from initiation of flushing till fruit set, is crucial to initiate timely management measures. . Field sanitation: Weeds like Mikania cordata, Bidens biternata, Emillia sp., Polygonum cltinese and Lantana camara offer excellent hiding places and serve as alternate host for the tea mosquito bug. Growth of weeds and wild host plants near in and around tea fiel{ may be controlled and this will help to reduce the growth of tea mosquito bug population. a Removal of the alternate host. o When an attack by Helopeltis becomes unmanageable the affected bushes mav be skiffed to reduced the damage. Regular survey and monitoring its population from initiation is crucial to initiate timely management measures. O Regulate the shade in densely shaded areas lopping of the lower branches of shade trees. a Ants, praying mantis, spiders and reduviids also help in minimizing TMB population to a certain extent. Hence, conservation of these biocontrol agents is helpful. Use of pesticides should be need based and it should be used as per label claim approved by the Registration Committee. Detailed information is available on Directorate's website in the link http //ppqs. gov. inldivisions/cib-rc/maj or-uses-of-pesticides.",443,en,2023.0
Advisory on management of Tea Mosquito Bug (Helopeltis theivora) in Tea crop.pdf_001,ppqs_advisories,Advisory on management of Tea Mosquito Bug (Helopeltis theivora) in Tea crop.pdf,1,"Hence, conservation of these biocontrol agents is helpful. Use of pesticides should be need based and it should be used as per label claim approved by the Registration Committee. Detailed information is available on Directorate's website in the link http //ppqs. gov. inldivisions/cib-rc/maj or-uses-of-pesticides. : Need based application of Bifenthrin 08 % SC @ 500 ml in 400 litre of waterlhectare, Clothianidrn 50% WDG @ 120 g in 500 litre of waterlhectare or Thiacloprid2L.T% SC@375 ml in 400 litre of waterlhectare or Thiamethoxam 25% WG @ 100 g in 400-500 litre of water/hectare can be advised if pest attain economic threshold level (ETL) Further the following strategies should also be adopted for spreading awareness among farmers: a Mobilization of extension functionaries at grass route level for regular monitoring of pests stafus. o Awareness among farming community through newspapers, printing and electronic media, Kisan Gosthi, leaflet and pamphlets. . The concerned officials at the district/ division/ block level may be advised to take the suitable plant protection measures immediately The detailed information on pests' incidence may a1s0 be sent to the directorate through emeil ppa@nic.in or appa.ipm-dppqs@,gov.in urgently to apprise the same to Department of Agriculture & Farmers' Welfare. Yours faithfully Encl: as above Plant Protection Adviser Copy for information: 1. PPS to Additional Secretary (PP), DA & FW, Krishi Bhawan, New Delhi, 1 10001 2. PPS to Joint Secretary (PP), DA & FW, Krishi Bhawan, New Delhi- 110001 3. The Director of Agriculture of other Tea growing states (Tamil Nadu, Assam, Kerala and West Bengal) with the request to keep close watch on pest build up and take appropriate measures as mentioned above. 4. PPS to PPA for information. 5.Officer-in-charges all CIPMCs of Tea growing states (Tamil Nadu, Assam, Kerala and West Bengal) with a direction to conduct regular survey in their areas to ascertain the pest incidence level and necessary follow-up with the state authorities. l,",317,en,2023.0
