Advisory on sucking pests of cotton crop.pdf_000,ppqs_advisories,Advisory on sucking pests of cotton crop.pdf,0,"ffi @ @ Phone:0129-2413985 E-mail:-ppa@nic.in rt{ 6<a €€ft El qtr F.No 3{/2022-23lIPIWAdvisorv qT<RFGFIV Government of India Tft \tri ftII|{ fdqrvr ri?|{q / Ministry of Ag culture & Farmers welfare lR \r?i fter{ 6'€alqr frr{F[ / Department of Agriculture & Famers Welfare T{wR Ti{qur, {Tnq qi ffiicrrdq / Directorate of Plant Protection, Quarantine & storag€ c{sq4, Fft'{F{rE€ftql1'rD - l2l 001/NH-lv, Faridabad (Haryana) - 121 001 Dated: ot.08.2022 To The Directors of Agdculture Punjab, Haryana, Rajasthan, Madhya Pradesh, Tamil Nadu, Karnataka, Guj arat, Maharashtra, Andhra Pradesh and Telanagana Sub: Advisory on sucking pests of cotton crop -reg. )1r, I have the honor to bring to your notice that sucking pests of cotton viz, jassids (Amrasca biguttula biguttula), aphids (Aphis gosslpii), whiteflies (Bemisia tabaci) and tltrips, (Thrips tabacl are major pests in cotton crop. Some times mites, mealy bug (Phenacoccus solenopsis), red cotton bug Q)ysdercus cingulatus), are also responsible for yield loss ifpopulation attain ETL level. Various sucking pests directly feed on the tender leaves and cause damage to the host, and plant becomes stunted, distorted, yellowish and show reduced strength and premature leaf loss apart from transmitting disease organisms. Keeping in view the current crop stage and prevailing weather conditions population of these pests are expected to increase . Therefore, cotton crop should be monitored regularly for pest build up and adopt Integrated Pest Management techniques. Agro Ecosystem Analysis (AESA) based IPM Package of practices of cotton are available at DPPQ&S website in the link http://ppqs.gov.in/ipm-packages .Important IPM practices for management of sucking pests are listed below o Regular surveillance on host crops including altemate hosts such as brinjal, cucurbits, cucumbers, long melon, Pumpkin, tomato, chilli, oka should be canied out for timely management of sucking pests. o Eradicate altemate weed host like Parthenium, Sida sp., Abutilon indicum, Datura metel, Xanthium sp, etc. to check the multiplication of sucking pests in off season. . Grow less preferred host crops like Maize, Bajra, Jowar and Dhecha"" etc. surrounding to the cotton field. o Balanced use of fprtilizers & optimum use of iffigation for overall crop pest artd disease management . Farmers must be educated to identiff, conserve and augment the natural enemies available in the cotton fieids parasitoides like Eretmocerus mundus, Encarsia formosa.,and predators like Spiders, Syrphid fly, Chrysoperla, Lady bird beetle, Dragon and Damsel flies, Praying mantis, staphylenids, Predatory ants, bugs & wasps are helpful to suppress the white fly population' . The predators viz., Chilomenes sexmaculatus and Chrysoperla carnea offer significant control of the early season sucking pests. A predatory prey ralio of 1.5 in respect ofjassids and 0.1 for aphids was found optimal for natural control in presence of coccinellids and chrysopids. . Use of yellow sticky traps @ 40 per acre during initial phase of the cotton crop to check the early infestation of sucking pests. . Use of 50lo Neem Seed Kemal Extract (NSKE) against white fly. ' w Az a a te d r i r o a r c h A ti z n a d 0 i .",503,en,2022.0
